Agent: I couldn't find an email or note in your inbox that says what you learned last night or that describes what you added to a Thai curry. I can search more thoroughly (other folders or a specific date) — or I can give likely recipe additions that give Thai curry a flavorful kick right away. Which would you like?

If you want me to search your mail, pick one:
- Search all folders (inbox, outbox, drafts) for "learned last night", "note", or "thoughts" — or a phrase like "Thai curry", "curry recipe", "added to curry".
- Search by date: tell me the date for "last night" and I’ll search emails from that day.
- Search for emails from a specific sender (if you emailed yourself or someone about it).

If you want cooking help now, here are common additions and techniques that give Thai curry a great kick (quick reference):
- Nam prik pao (Thai roasted chili paste) — 1 tbsp added while sautéing the curry paste gives smoky, sweet heat.  
- Fish sauce — 1–2 tsp for salty umami depth.  
- Palm sugar (or brown sugar) — 1 tsp to balance heat and salt.  
- Kaffir lime leaves (torn) or lime zest — bright citrus aroma.  
- Fresh lime juice — squeeze at the end for brightness.  
- Thai basil (or holy basil) — handful stirred in at the end for fragrance.  
- Fresh chilies or roasted dried chilies — adjust for more heat.  
- Galangal or lemongrass (sliced) — aromatic complexity if you have them.  
- Toasted coconut cream (browning a bit of the coconut milk first) — adds richness and depth.  
- A pinch of ground turmeric or a dash of tamarind paste — for earthiness/tang (optional).  

